<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yaroslav of Tver</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1230">1230</a>–<a href="/years/1271">1271</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Yaroslav III of Vladimir, Yaroslav Yaroslavich · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yaroslav Yaroslavich was the first Prince of Tver, from about 1247, and Grand Prince of Vladimir from 1264 to 1271. A younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a>, he founded the dynasty that ruled Tver until 1485 and as grand prince clashed with Novgorod, which accepted him only under written conditions. He died returning from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> in 1271.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yaroslav Yaroslavich was born in 1230, a son of Yaroslav II and a younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a>. In 1247 he received the town of Tver from his uncle. From this small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> he and his son Mikhail presided over Tver's transformation into one of the principal centres of power in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, which would later compete with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. All the later princes of Tver descended from him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1252 Yaroslav and his brother Andrey seized Alexander's capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl-Zalessky</a>. Alexander, reinforced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> units, fought it back, taking Yaroslav's children prisoner and leaving Yaroslav's wife dead on the field of battle. Yaroslav fled to Ladoga. In 1255 he became prince of Novgorod after Alexander's son Vasily was expelled, but Alexander returned to the city, dismissed the posadnik, and by the next year Vasily had been sent back to reign. In 1258 Yaroslav visited the khan's capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a>, and two years later he led the Novgorod army against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>.</p><p>When Alexander died in 1263, Yaroslav quarrelled with Andrey over who should become grand prince. They went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> for arbitration, which was decided in Yaroslav's favour. He nevertheless settled in Novgorod and married a daughter of a local boyar. Various Novgorodian factions still conspired against him and sought to place on the throne either his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-of-kostroma">Vasily of Kostroma</a> or Alexander's son Dmitri of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1270 the armies of three princes stood for a week near the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staraya-russa">Staraya Russa</a>, ready for battle, until the metropolitan managed to reconcile them. Yaroslav surrendered Novgorod to his nephew and accompanied him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a>. He died on his way back to Tver on 9 September 1271. His eldest son Svyatoslav succeeded him in Tver, and Svyatoslav was followed by Mikhail.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yaroslav of Tver” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tver, on the upper Volga, received its first prince around 1247, when <b>Yaroslav Yaroslavich</b>, a younger brother of Alexander Nevsky, made it his seat.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-principality-of-tver">Grand Principality of Tver</a></li></ul></section>
